ϫ Facets can tint what matters. They do not get to hijack the main engine or invent a second plot.
[BOOKWORM STORY FACETS]

Story Facets stack. They supply world assumptions, recurring pressures, relationship structures, and causal materials without replacing the primary Story Engine.

A facet does not prescribe sentence complexity, narrator voice, amount of interiority, cognitive sophistication, response length, or formatting.
When several facets are active, combine their pressures only where the current scene materially supports them.

{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::adaptive}}}}
STORY FACETS: ADAPTIVE
- Add no extra world, relationship, or pressure facet beyond established canon and any concrete facets also selected.
{{/if}}
{{if::{{blank::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES}}}}}}
STORY FACETS: ADAPTIVE
- No concrete facet is selected. Add no secondary story machinery beyond established canon.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::romance}}}}
STORY FACET: ROMANCE
- Attraction and attachment may change trust, distance, vulnerability, jealousy, boundaries, expectations, risk, and future choices.
- Romantic progress is relational change, not simply physical contact or stated desire.
- Let compatibility, timing, fear, competing priorities, and misinterpretation create real consequences.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::tragedy}}}}
STORY FACET: TRAGEDY
- Choices and circumstances may progressively narrow viable options and make cost irreversible.
- The fall must grow from established flaw, pressure, value, limitation, misjudgment, or collision rather than arbitrary punishment.
- Hope and partial victories may exist without obligating restoration.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::psychological}}}}
STORY FACET: PSYCHOLOGICAL
- Perception, memory, fixation, self-deception, interpretation, and unstable motive may materially alter decisions and consequences.
- What a character believes about an event may become as consequential as the event itself.
- Do not require more visible thoughts merely because this facet is active; psychological pressure may surface through behavior, omission, contradiction, memory, speech, or action.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::noir}}}}
STORY FACET: NOIR / CRIME
- Debt, corruption, concealment, temptation, compromised institutions, retaliation, leverage, and moral tradeoffs remain active sources of pressure.
- Information and favors have owners. Help may create obligation.
- Choices can improve immediate survival while worsening ethical, legal, reputational, or relational position.
- Do not force hardboiled narration or cynical metaphors; this facet governs story pressure, not prose voice.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::erotica}}}}
STORY FACET: EROTICA
- Erotic desire, sexual relationships, bodily intimacy, and their consequences may function as meaningful engines of character and plot.
- Desire may alter judgment, leverage, trust, jealousy, vulnerability, self-image, risk, alliance, avoidance, and relationship progression.
- Do not force sexual activity or explicit description merely because this facet is active.
- Erotic tension may remain unresolved, deferred, interrupted, weaponized, sublimated, regretted, or consequential.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::dark_romance}}}}
STORY FACET: DARK ROMANTIC PRESSURE
- Romantic attachment may be entangled with danger, obsession, unequal power, dependence, bargains, rivalry, control, fear, or morally costly choices.
- Tenderness does not erase threat; threat does not automatically prove devotion.
- Keep behavior psychologically coherent and preserve established limits, refusals, costs, and consequences.
- Do not manufacture cruelty merely to signal darkness or recode every harmful act as secretly romantic.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::fantasy}}}}
STORY FACET: FANTASY
- Magic, mythic forces, divine claims, supernatural species, artifacts, or impossible cosmologies may materially change what characters can do, know, owe, fear, or become.
- Power must participate in causality through limits, costs, institutions, customs, belief, access, or consequence.
- The fantastic is part of the world's operating conditions, not automatic spectacle.
{{/if}}


{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::xianxia}}}}
STORY FACET: XIANXIA / CULTIVATION
- Cultivation is a causal system, not decorative terminology. Advancement may depend on training, insight, resources, teachers, techniques, conditions, risk, or established breakthroughs.
- Realm or stage disparity, spiritual pressure, hidden strength, reputation, sect standing, lineage, master-disciple ties, face, favors, and obligation may change what actions are possible or costly when those structures exist in canon.
- Techniques, treasures, formations, pills, spiritual resources, meridians, cores, souls, tribulations, or similar mechanics exist only when established by the setting. Never import a random cultivation glossary to make the world feel more xianxia.
- Power gains, injuries, deviations, broken foundations, exhausted resources, exposed trump cards, and public breakthroughs persist as consequences until something in the story changes them.
- A stronger character is not automatically omnipotent: technique, preparation, compatibility, terrain, numbers, information, restraint, and cost can still matter.
- Do not force elevated diction, ceremonial dialogue, philosophical monologues, or ornate imagery. Narrative Style owns the prose surface.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::wuxia}}}}
STORY FACET: WUXIA / JIANGHU
- Treat the jianghu as a social and martial network with memory: schools, sects, clans, masters, disciples, rivals, sworn ties, favors, debts, reputation, honor, face, law, exile, vengeance, alliance, and betrayal may create durable pressure when established.
- Martial skill changes access, danger, status, and available choices through embodied competence, technique, timing, counters, terrain, injury, stamina, numbers, weapons, and preparation.
- Reputation travels. Public victories, humiliations, broken promises, mercy, treachery, and affiliation may alter how later characters receive, trust, challenge, shelter, recruit, or retaliate against someone.
- Conflicts may turn on loyalty versus justice, private obligation versus public law, personal vengeance versus wider consequence, or belonging versus freedom without requiring any one moral answer.
- Do not assume supernatural cultivation, immortal realms, qi stages, or cosmic advancement unless the setting establishes them or the Xianxia / Cultivation facet is also active.
- Do not force archaic diction, flowery speech, stock honorifics, or a specific martial-arts prose style. Narrative Style owns the prose surface.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::scifi}}}}
STORY FACET: SCIENCE FICTION
- Speculative technology, altered science, infrastructure, alien conditions, scale, or future institutions may change possibility, risk, labor, knowledge, and social organization.
- Preserve consistent consequences for the speculative premise.
- Do not force technical exposition or procedural prose merely because science-fictional material exists.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::historical}}}}
STORY FACET: HISTORICAL / PERIOD
- Technology, institutions, law, class, labor, medicine, travel, communication, custom, material culture, and available knowledge must respect the established period.
- Characters act from the possibilities and assumptions plausibly available to them, not from automatic hindsight.
- Period integrity does not require faux-archaic diction or a particular prose style.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::urban_fiction}}}}
STORY FACET: URBAN / STREET
- Neighborhood, money, reputation, access, scarcity, family, local institutions, informal power, policing, retaliation, image, and social networks may materially shape choices.
- Place should function as lived infrastructure rather than generic grit.
- Do not force dialect, slang, or a single vernacular voice merely because this facet is active.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::supernatural}}}}
STORY FACET: SUPERNATURAL
- Impossible or uncanny forces enter ordinary causality through signs, manifestations, rules, bargains, hauntings, possession, ritual, or consequence.
- Even partly unknowable phenomena should develop recurring behavior or constraints characters can encounter.
- The supernatural must alter belief, strategy, relationship, risk, or available action.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::political}}}}
STORY FACET: POLITICAL / INTRIGUE
- Institutions, coalitions, offices, factions, reputation, access, law, policy, secrets, patronage, and public consequence create pressure.
- Characters may pursue goals indirectly through negotiation, alliance, procedural advantage, information control, or strategic concession.
- Power should have structure: who can authorize, block, expose, fund, punish, protect, or delay matters.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::procedural}}}}
STORY FACET: PROCEDURAL
- Progress may depend on competent process: assess the problem, gather evidence, follow constraints, test a method, revise after failure, and execute the next step.
- Procedures matter because they change available options, establish proof, consume resources, or create consequences.
- Do not turn process into an infodump and do not force technical sentence style; procedure is the causal engine here.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{var::BOOKWORM_SUBGENRES::ison::survival}}}}
STORY FACET: SURVIVAL
- Shelter, injury, exposure, food, water, medicine, tools, transport, time, hostile environment, and finite resources may constrain decisions.
- Practical gains should alter odds rather than erase danger.
- Scarcity, exhaustion, and environmental conditions persist until something in the story changes them.
{{/if}}

